A||||12||HAGS||Charging Question||||5||
Z||000000||HAGS||01-25-2001||05:23 PM||||Do I ground the jumper cable connection to my battery if im using the donor car to charge it?  Im looking to charge my battery not jump start the car.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||01-25-2001||06:06 PM||||I would stay away from charging another battery like this.  When ever I jump start a car I hook the cables to the posts on the live car. Then on the dead one hook up to the hot post, with the negative connection I then connect to the engine block somewhere on steel.  The reason, simple, is that the gases escaping from the battery can ignite when you  hook both connections to the posts, causing a nice ball of flames.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||HAGS||01-25-2001||08:08 PM||||well I took it to the shop to get it replaced on warranty, instead they charged it up...I make is 3 miles and my engine stalled, kept trying to crank it and it wouldnt start, after about the 12th try it wouldnt even crank, the battery was dead...I put a new alternator in yesterday.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||01-25-2001||08:10 PM||||way better off buying a little $10 trickle charger. either that or switch batteries and charge it that way.||||reg||1
Z||000004||HAGS||01-25-2001||08:13 PM||||well I was waiting at the shop for atleast 45 minutes while they charged it....it read 1.8volts before I took it, myself and friends agreed that it was toast but I took it to the shop and they said they could charge it....it died....im not sure if the battery caused the stall though because it was cranking normally for awhile, just wouldnt start.||||reg||1
